1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,604
Azure Migrate helps customers understand and modernize their on-premises estate.

2
00:00:03,604 --> 00:00:07,930
In the AI era, we have an opportunity to extend that journey.

3
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:12,255
Not only helping customers decide how to move an application to Azure,

4
00:00:12,255 --> 00:00:16,580
but also helping them identify where AI can create meaningful value, and

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:19,824
what it would take to deliver that value responsibly.

6
00:00:20,824 --> 00:00:23,133
Azure Migrate begins with discovery.

7
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:28,213
It prepares an inventory of virtual machines, web applications, databases, file

8
00:00:28,213 --> 00:00:30,522
shares, containers, and supporting dependencies.

9
00:00:30,522 --> 00:00:36,063
Customers can then organize these components as a complete application, a unit

10
00:00:36,063 --> 00:00:38,372
that works and moves together.

11
00:00:38,372 --> 00:00:42,529
Assessment maps each component to an appropriate Azure target.

12
00:00:42,529 --> 00:00:46,685
A web application might map to Azure App Service.

13
00:00:46,685 --> 00:00:50,841
SQL Server might map to Azure SQL Managed Instance.

14
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:54,997
A containerized workload might map to Azure Kubernetes Service.

15
00:00:54,997 --> 00:00:57,768
This gives us rich application intelligence:

16
00:00:57,768 --> 00:01:01,924
the application boundary, runtime, dependencies, assessed targets, readiness, and

17
00:01:01,924 --> 00:01:02,848
cost context.

18
00:01:03,848 --> 00:01:07,213
That assessment is excellent at answering the cloud-era question:

19
00:01:07,213 --> 00:01:10,577
where and how should this application run in Azure?

20
00:01:10,577 --> 00:01:13,194
But customers increasingly ask a second question:

21
00:01:13,194 --> 00:01:16,558
how can I use AI in this existing application?

22
00:01:16,558 --> 00:01:21,044
And, if I already use AI on-premises, how should I modernize that

23
00:01:21,044 --> 00:01:22,166
capability in Azure?

24
00:01:22,166 --> 00:01:24,409
This leads to two strategic questions.

25
00:01:24,409 --> 00:01:28,895
How do we help customers find credible AI opportunities in the applications

26
00:01:28,895 --> 00:01:30,016
they already own?

27
00:01:30,016 --> 00:01:34,502
And what should the next generation of Azure Migrate look like in

28
00:01:34,502 --> 00:01:35,624
the AI era?

29
00:01:36,624 --> 00:01:41,958
The proposed idea is an AI Feasibility Assessment as a new step

30
00:01:41,958 --> 00:01:44,180
in the Azure Migrate journey.

31
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:44,625
Discover.

32
00:01:44,625 --> 00:01:45,069
Assess.

33
00:01:45,069 --> 00:01:46,403
AI Feasibility Assessment.

34
00:01:46,403 --> 00:01:46,847
Design.

35
00:01:46,847 --> 00:01:47,292
Estimate.

36
00:01:47,292 --> 00:01:47,736
Modernize.

37
00:01:47,736 --> 00:01:53,070
It adds an AI modernization layer on top of existing application intelligence.

38
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:58,404
The goal is not to turn every application into an AI project.

39
00:01:58,404 --> 00:02:03,738
The goal is to identify which application and workflow combinations justify a

40
00:02:03,738 --> 00:02:09,072
bounded experiment, which need remediation first, and which should not pursue AI.

41
00:02:10,072 --> 00:02:14,233
For each selected application, the assessment should answer four practical

42
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:14,649
questions.

43
00:02:14,649 --> 00:02:18,394
Where can AI create meaningful business or engineering value?

44
00:02:18,394 --> 00:02:22,971
Is the application and its data technically ready for that scenario?

45
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:27,132
Which integrations, Azure services, and security or governance controls are

46
00:02:27,132 --> 00:02:27,548
required?

47
00:02:27,548 --> 00:02:32,542
And what is the pragmatic path from proof of concept, to pilot,

48
00:02:32,542 --> 00:02:33,374
to production?

49
00:02:33,374 --> 00:02:38,367
The output is a reviewable decision pack with explicit evidence gaps and

50
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:42,112
production gates, not a generic list of AI ideas.

51
00:02:43,112 --> 00:02:46,087
The experience combines two evidence streams.

52
00:02:46,087 --> 00:02:49,061
Azure Migrate contributes observed application facts:

53
00:02:49,061 --> 00:02:53,028
components, dependencies, runtime, assessed targets, and migration findings.

54
00:02:53,028 --> 00:02:56,002
The customer contributes the business meaning:

55
00:02:56,002 --> 00:03:01,456
the process to improve, users, desired outcome, data constraints, compliance needs,

56
00:03:01,456 --> 00:03:02,943
and expected usage.

57
00:03:02,943 --> 00:03:08,397
The assessment then produces a feasibility summary, data and integration readiness,

58
00:03:08,397 --> 00:03:12,859
governance considerations, recommended Azure AI services, indicative cost scenarios,

59
00:03:12,859 --> 00:03:17,817
an AI-ready architecture extension, and a proof of concept roadmap.

60
00:03:17,817 --> 00:03:20,792
The key design principle is explainability.

61
00:03:20,792 --> 00:03:24,758
Observed facts, customer-provided context, inferred assumptions, and missing

62
00:03:24,758 --> 00:03:26,741
evidence remain visibly separate.

63
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:30,707
Rules and deterministic calculations control the decision pack.

64
00:03:30,707 --> 00:03:36,657
Generative AI can improve the narrative, but it does not silently invent

65
00:03:36,657 --> 00:03:38,144
readiness or cost.

66
00:03:39,144 --> 00:03:43,023
Let’s see the proposed experience in the Azure Migrate simulator.

67
00:03:43,023 --> 00:03:45,351
Discovery and assessment are already complete.

68
00:03:45,351 --> 00:03:50,006
We will start with the B P T co application, inspect its

69
00:03:50,006 --> 00:03:53,885
current Azure architecture, capture a document-processing outcome, generate an AI

70
00:03:53,885 --> 00:03:58,540
Feasibility Assessment, and then see exactly where the proposed AI services fit

71
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:00,480
in the application landing zone.

72
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:06,482
Discovery and assessment are already complete, so we move directly to Plan.

73
00:04:06,482 --> 00:04:10,651
Under Plan, Application Groups brings related infrastructure and workloads together

74
00:04:10,651 --> 00:04:11,901
as one application.

75
00:04:11,901 --> 00:04:16,903
B P T co includes its virtual machines and the discovered web,

76
00:04:16,903 --> 00:04:21,072
database, container, orchestration, and file-share components that belong to the

77
00:04:21,072 --> 00:04:21,905
application boundary.

78
00:04:21,905 --> 00:04:24,823
This is the scope we carry forward.

79
00:04:24,823 --> 00:04:28,575
AI feasibility is not assessed against an isolated server.

80
00:04:28,575 --> 00:04:33,160
It is assessed against the complete application and a customer-confirmed workflow.

81
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:38,890
First, we look at the current proposed Azure architecture with AI turned

82
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:39,284
off.

83
00:04:39,284 --> 00:04:43,620
This is the application landing-zone view produced from the migration assessment.

84
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:47,562
It shows secure ingress, network boundaries, shared platform services, assessed

85
00:04:47,562 --> 00:04:50,321
application targets, and data or storage dependencies.

86
00:04:50,321 --> 00:04:55,052
At this point, the design answers where and how B P T

87
00:04:55,052 --> 00:04:57,023
co could run in Azure.

88
00:04:57,023 --> 00:05:00,176
It does not yet add an AI capability.

89
00:05:01,176 --> 00:05:06,264
Now the customer wants to explore an AI opportunity for the same

90
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:06,688
application.

91
00:05:06,688 --> 00:05:10,504
In Decide, the Assessment experience includes AI Feasibility Assessment.

92
00:05:10,504 --> 00:05:15,592
We select B P T co, then choose the broad solution idea:

93
00:05:15,592 --> 00:05:17,288
Document Processing and Enrichment.

94
00:05:17,288 --> 00:05:22,376
These categories guide the assessment; they do not predetermine a service purchase

95
00:05:22,376 --> 00:05:25,344
or claim that the scenario is feasible.

96
00:05:26,344 --> 00:05:30,697
The customer now supplies the context discovery cannot infer.

97
00:05:30,697 --> 00:05:36,501
Here the process is document intake and enrichment across invoices, contracts, and

98
00:05:36,501 --> 00:05:36,985
attachments.

99
00:05:36,985 --> 00:05:41,338
The intended capability is classification, field extraction, metadata enrichment,

100
00:05:41,338 --> 00:05:45,207
summarization, business-rule validation, and human review for exceptions.

101
00:05:45,207 --> 00:05:47,625
The business outcome is explicit:

102
00:05:47,625 --> 00:05:53,429
reduce manual data entry by sixty percent, reduce processing time by fifty

103
00:05:53,429 --> 00:05:58,750
percent, and target ninety-five percent accuracy for critical fields, while keeping

104
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,168
controlled access, approval, and auditability.

105
00:06:02,168 --> 00:06:05,288
The customer has confirmed the processing boundary.

106
00:06:05,288 --> 00:06:10,190
The entered intent, expected outcome, a deterministic local draft, and minimized

107
00:06:10,190 --> 00:06:14,646
evidence labels may be sent to the configured Foundry model.

108
00:06:14,646 --> 00:06:18,657
Uploaded content is treated as data, not as instructions.

109
00:06:18,657 --> 00:06:21,776
The result is a versioned decision pack.

110
00:06:21,776 --> 00:06:26,233
It separates business fit, data readiness, integration readiness, security and

111
00:06:26,233 --> 00:06:28,907
governance, delivery and operations, and economics.

112
00:06:28,907 --> 00:06:33,809
For this scenario, the proposed capability set includes Azure AI Document

113
00:06:33,809 --> 00:06:38,265
Intelligence for extraction and classification, Azure AI Search for permission-aware

114
00:06:38,265 --> 00:06:42,722
retrieval, a Microsoft Foundry model for grounded enrichment and summarization,

115
00:06:42,722 --> 00:06:45,395
workflow orchestration, approved storage, and monitoring.

116
00:06:45,395 --> 00:06:50,743
The estimate is shown as low, base, and high scenarios with explicit

117
00:06:50,743 --> 00:06:52,526
assumptions and a reserve.

118
00:06:52,526 --> 00:06:57,873
The output also identifies evidence gaps and defines the smallest bounded proof

119
00:06:57,873 --> 00:07:02,330
of concept and the tests required before any production decision.

120
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:06,786
A proof of concept candidate means justified experimentation, not production

121
00:07:06,786 --> 00:07:07,232
readiness.

122
00:07:08,232 --> 00:07:12,575
From the assessment we move directly into the AI-ready architecture.

123
00:07:12,575 --> 00:07:17,786
The original application design is preserved, but the diagram is replaced with

124
00:07:17,786 --> 00:07:20,826
a reviewed AI-ready variant for this application.

125
00:07:20,826 --> 00:07:25,168
The new capability extension shows where document intelligence, search, model

126
00:07:25,168 --> 00:07:29,077
access, orchestration, approved storage, and observability fit alongside the

127
00:07:29,077 --> 00:07:31,248
assessed application and data services.

128
00:07:31,248 --> 00:07:34,722
Every AI node is visibly marked as proposed.

129
00:07:34,722 --> 00:07:39,933
The diagram links back to the exact assessment revision, and base application

130
00:07:39,933 --> 00:07:43,842
cost remains separate from the incremental AI planning budget.

131
00:07:43,842 --> 00:07:49,053
If the checkbox is turned off, the architecture returns to the original

132
00:07:49,053 --> 00:07:49,921
migration design.

133
00:07:49,921 --> 00:07:54,264
AI is an explicit, assessed extension, not a silent default.

134
00:07:55,264 --> 00:07:59,336
This demonstration connects four things that are usually separated.

135
00:07:59,336 --> 00:08:01,598
It reuses existing application intelligence.

136
00:08:01,598 --> 00:08:05,218
It captures the customer’s business intent and outcome.

137
00:08:05,218 --> 00:08:08,385
It produces an explainable, evidence-backed decision pack.

138
00:08:08,385 --> 00:08:13,815
And it carries the approved scenario into an AI-ready architecture without losing

139
00:08:13,815 --> 00:08:15,625
the base application design.

140
00:08:15,625 --> 00:08:21,054
The result is a qualified modernization conversation and a practical handoff, not

141
00:08:21,054 --> 00:08:22,864
a generic AI brainstorm.

142
00:08:23,864 --> 00:08:25,696
The larger vision is simple.

143
00:08:25,696 --> 00:08:27,895
Azure Migrate has traditionally helped answer:

144
00:08:27,895 --> 00:08:30,826
how do I move this application to Azure?

145
00:08:30,826 --> 00:08:34,124
In the AI era, it can also help answer:

146
00:08:34,124 --> 00:08:38,521
how can I transform this application with AI, and what would it

147
00:08:38,521 --> 00:08:39,987
take to get there?

148
00:08:39,987 --> 00:08:41,452
Migration remains the foundation.

149
00:08:41,452 --> 00:08:45,849
AI Feasibility adds the next modernization decision at exactly the point where

150
00:08:45,849 --> 00:08:49,880
the customer already understands the application and is choosing its future.

151
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:56,309
Azure Migrate already works through a broad ecosystem, including C M F,

152
00:08:56,309 --> 00:09:01,286
S A Desk, cloud solution architects, systems integrators, and other delivery

153
00:09:01,286 --> 00:09:01,738
partners.

154
00:09:01,738 --> 00:09:06,262
AI Feasibility can extend those relationships with a better-qualified application

155
00:09:06,262 --> 00:09:08,524
opportunity and a versioned handoff.

156
00:09:08,524 --> 00:09:13,953
It can also create new collaboration paths with Microsoft Foundry, Azure Frontier,

157
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:17,120
AI field engineers, and Forward Deployed Engineers.

158
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:20,287
Azure Migrate owns the application-to-opportunity decision experience.

159
00:09:20,287 --> 00:09:24,359
Platform and delivery teams validate and build the solution.

160
00:09:24,359 --> 00:09:29,336
The customer retains ownership of business value, data authorization, and production

161
00:09:29,336 --> 00:09:29,788
acceptance.

162
00:09:29,788 --> 00:09:33,407
The next step is a bounded design-partner validation:

163
00:09:33,407 --> 00:09:37,479
human-reviewed recommendations, clear evidence gates, and measurable movement from

164
00:09:37,479 --> 00:09:40,646
qualified opportunity to customer-owned proof of concept.

165
00:09:40,646 --> 00:09:43,813
Azure Migrate helps us understand the application.

166
00:09:43,813 --> 00:09:49,242
AI Feasibility helps the customer decide how that application can create new

167
00:09:49,242 --> 00:09:51,504
value in the AI era.
